<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Quetzalcoatl</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Feathered Serpent, Kukulkan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Quetzalcoatl, &quot;feathered serpent&quot;, is a Mesoamerican deity associated with wind, learning, priesthood and the morning star. Images of a feathered serpent appear at Teotihuacan by the 3rd century CE, and the god was later linked with the legendary Toltec priest-king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/topiltzin-quetzalcoatl">Topiltzin Quetzalcoatl</a>. Cholula was his chief pilgrimage centre, and among the Maya he was known as Kukulkan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/quetzalcoatl.jpg" alt="Quetzalcoatl" width="320" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Quetzalc%C3%B3atl_como_la_serpiente_emplumada_y_el_dios_del_viento_Eh%C3%A9catl,_en_el_folio_19.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> and means Precious serpent or Quetzal-feathered Serpent. The earliest known depiction of a feathered serpent is on Stela 19 at the Olmec site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-venta">La Venta</a>, dated to around 900 BCE, where a serpent rises behind a person who is probably engaged in a shamanic ritual. It is probably not the same deity as the later one, but it shows that feathered snakes were a symbol in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a> from the Formative period on.</p><p>Teotihuacan was the first culture to use the feathered serpent as an important religious and political symbol, notably at the Quetzalcoatl temple in the Ciudadela complex. The earliest images show a real snake; among the Classic Maya the figure began to take human features, such as a beard. After the fall of Teotihuacan, around 600 CE, the cult spread to Xochicalco, Cacaxtla and Cholula, and images then appear at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichen-itza">Chichén Itzá</a> and El Tajín. Cholula remained the chief centre of his worship in the Postclassic period.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Among the Aztecs Quetzalcoatl was a creator deity linked to wind, the planet Venus, the Sun, merchants, crafts and learning, and the patron of the priesthood. The two chief priests of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/templo-mayor">Templo Mayor</a> bore the title Quetzalcoatl Tlamacazqui. As Ehecatl he is the wind; as the morning star he is called Tlahuizcalpantecuhtli, and his twin Xolotl is the evening star.</p><p>He descended to Mictlan, the underworld, and made the people of the fifth world from the bones of earlier races, using blood drawn from his own body. He is credited with inventing books and the calendar and with giving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> to humankind. In the Codex Chimalpopoca, Tezcatlipoca makes him drunk on pulque; ashamed, he burns himself in a stone chest, and his heart rises as the morning star.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Since the sixteenth century it has been widely held that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moctezuma-ii">Moctezuma II</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a>, landing in 1519, for the returning Quetzalcoatl. Many scholars, among them Matthew Restall and James Lockhart, regard this as one of the myths of the Spanish conquest. They note that it appears only in documents of Spanish influence, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florentine-codex">Florentine Codex</a>, written some fifty years after the conquest. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a> such as Toribio de Benavente equated the god with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-the-apostle">Thomas the Apostle</a>.</p><p>Between 1200 and 1600 a confederacy of Eastern Nahuas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mixtecs">Mixtecs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zapotecs">Zapotecs</a> kept a pilgrimage and commercial centre at Cholula, which the Spaniards compared to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>. It fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a> for nearly seventy-five years and supplied the Spaniards with an army.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Quetzalcōātl” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Murals in compounds such as Tepantitla and Tetitla depict a storm god related to the later Tlaloc, the <b>Feathered Serpent</b> and a female deity often called the Great Goddess, and figurines and incense burners reveal household ritual.</blockquote><a href="/empire/teotihuacan">Teotihuacan · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Colonial chronicles say that the Toltecs combined Tolteca-Chichimeca migrants from the north with the Nonoalca, a people associated with the Gulf Coast, and they credit the city’s rise to leaders such as Mixcoatl and his son Ce Acatl Topiltzin <b>Quetzalcoatl</b>, figures of legend rather than of history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/toltec-state">Toltec state · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…and Chaak by the Maya, who lived in mountains and brought the rain on which maize depended; the <b>Feathered Serpent</b>, Quetzalcoatl to the Aztecs and K’uk’ulkan to the Maya of Yucatán, a god of wind, priesthood and learning; the sun god; the young maize god, whose death and…</blockquote><a href="/religion/mesoamerican-religion">Mesoamerican religion · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>There is some evidence that gods of other peoples were worshipped too, such as the <b>Feathered Serpent</b> of Teotihuacan and the Nahuatl god Xipe Totec.</blockquote><a href="/culture/zapotec-civilisation">Zapotec civilisation · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>The nobles (pipiltin) were born to their rank and claimed descent from the Toltecs and, through them, from the god <b>Quetzalcoatl</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/aztec-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Aztec Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>It was a famous pilgrimage centre of <b>Quetzalcoatl</b> and a market city, known for fine polychrome pottery.</blockquote><a href="/state/cholula">Cholula</a></li></ul></section>
